    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Nucleophosmin 1 (NPM1) mutations are implicated as the driver mutation in approximately one-third of adult acute myeloid leukemia (AML) patients. Despite the prevalence of this mutation, however, targeted therapies remain elusive for patients with this subtype of leukemia because the molecular mechanism of NPM1 mutations is unknown. The principle model is that mutant NPM1 (mutNPM1) leads to p53 degradation, allowing cells to escape from p53-mediated cell cycle arrest. However, the distinct clinical characteristics of AML with NPM1 mutations and AML with p53 mutations do not support a simple link between mutNPM1 and p53. This discrepancy stimulated our investigation of mutNPM1's involvement in alternative mechanisms for leukemogenesis. We first conducted a screen for novel binding partners of mutNPM1 through immunopurification of the protein complexes formed by wild-type (WT) NPM1 and mutNPM1 in human leukemia cells followed by mass spectrometry (MS) analysis. MutNPM1 exhibited novel or enriched binding to eukaryotic translation initiation factors (eIFs) compared to WT NPM1. These findings implicated a putative role of mutNPM1 in the upregulation of translation through direct interactions with the initiation machinery, consistent with the theory that cancer cells require aberrantly high levels of protein synthesis to sustain rapid proliferation. However, inability to validate these interactions through co-immunoprecipitation warrants further investigation of the molecular mechanism of mutNPM1 and how it promotes leukemogenesis, in order to potentially reveal unexplored therapeutic targets.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
